UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2210803323001677
KW  - Supplement
KW  - Safety evaluation trial
KW  - ETAS
KW  - Excessive dose trial
KW  - Cortisol
AB  - ABSTRACT
Introduction
With the increasing popularity of functional foods in recent years, there is a growing need for high-quality clinical trials. A standardised extract of Asparagus officinalis stem (ETAS) has been developed as a new functional material, which is expected to have stress-relieving effects. Although ETAS has been examined in clinical trials, there remain concerns about its safety, especially in terms of the potential for overdose and long-term use. To test the safety hypothesis, here, we performed a double-blind, placebo-controlled phase I and II trial to evaluate the assumption that ETAS can safely be taken by healthy subjects (a public trial registry No. UMIN000016582).
Methods
A total of 92 volunteers were recruited. After the screening, 55 subjects were included and allocated to the ETAS or placebo group. During the intervention, subjects were administered ETAS (1 500 mg as ETAS50 per day) or placebo for 28 days. Before and after the intervention, participants were evaluated by blood, urine, and anthropometric evaluations, and surveys. To assess effectiveness, we measured blood levels of cortisol, a stress indicator.
Results
The final analysis included 23 subjects in the placebo group and 23 subjects in the ETAS group. No subjects withdrew from the study due to difficulties taking the capsules. No severe side effects were observed in either group. Regarding the effectiveness, the blood cortisol level was significantly increased after the intervention only in the placebo group.
Conclusions
Our findings indicated that ETAS can be safely administered with minimal side effects and may have stress-relieving effects in healthy subjects.
